WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Hey everyone, and welcome to The Explainer. I am so glad you're here. Today, I'm going to be putting on my senior product manager mentor hat. We're going to look way past all the buzzwords and the theory, and I'm going to show you exactly how the real job is done. Look, if you want to build products that people actually need, products that fundamentally change user behavior and, you know, actually drive market success, you have to understand exactly who you are building for. So today, we're going to master the art and the science of building strategic personas. Let's get started.

2
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:36.040
into it. Okay, let's dive right into our syllabus for today's masterclass. We'll be moving through

3
00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:40.780
six structured steps, starting off with the cognitive bias trap. Then we'll move into

4
00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:46.480
building a strategic persona and uncovering jobs to be done. From there, we'll tackle mapping the

5
00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:51.100
persona journey, passing the mom test, and finally, balancing qualitative and quantitative

6
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:55.580
data. This is literally your roadmap to building products that are rooted in reality.

7
00:00:55.580 --> 00:01:01.750
All right, section one, the cognitive bias trap and exactly why you need research.

8
00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:07.490
Listen, before we build a single thing, we absolutely have to address the most dangerous

9
00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:12.150
mistake that junior product managers make right out of the gate. And that's assuming that you

10
00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:17.510
are the user. The crucial point is this. Assuming your personal experience represents the broader

11
00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:23.530
user base is a massive, massive cognitive bias. Like even if you use your own product every single

12
00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:28.230
day, your experience simply does not represent the market as a whole. Without user research,

13
00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:33.110
you aren't actually managing a product. You're really just guessing. Research is the absolute

14
00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:38.010
bedrock of every single product decision you're going to make. Now, to avoid gathering bad data,

15
00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:43.610
you have to be incredibly selective about who you actually talk to. You've got to target highly

16
00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:48.950
precise segments. So don't just say students. Say second year engineering students who work

17
00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:54.570
part-time. See the difference? Also, avoid your power users. I know it's tempting, but I already

18
00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:58.810
love your product and they're just going to give you skewed, overly positive feedback. You also

19
00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:04.350
want to ensure you have real diversity in roles, tenure, and company size. And honestly, here's the

20
00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:10.110
best part. You do not need to interview hundreds of people. Actually, scratch that. You really only

21
00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:15.570
need five to eight high-quality interviews to hit what we call a saturation point, which is where

22
00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:22.730
really clear patterns start to emerge. Let's move on to section two, building a strategic persona,

23
00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:29.210
moving beyond abstract users. So this is where we take all that great data we've gathered and

24
00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:34.510
turn it into the ultimate tool to anchor our decisions. Meet Sarah. She is not just some

25
00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:39.690
abstract statistic. She is a 32-year-old B2B SaaS team lead at a mid-sized tech company.

26
00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:44.510
And notice we aren't just listing demographic facts here. We are defining her specific goals,

27
00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:48.570
like wanting to keep her team productive, and we're pinpointing her exact pain points,

28
00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:53.490
like a total lack of visibility into her team's workloads. Giving Sarah a real face and a real

29
00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:58.650
story ensures that our product solves an actual human problem, and it keeps our entire strategy

30
00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:05.050
completely grounded. Which brings us to Section 3, Uncovering Jobs to be Done, or JTBD,

31
00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:10.970
the Engine of Intent. Now, this framework shifts our focus entirely. Instead of asking

32
00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:14.950
what our persona wants, we have to ask what they are actually trying to accomplish.

33
00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:18.930
It's this brilliant theory by Clayton Christensen, which basically states that

34
00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:23.790
customers don't buy products at all. They hire them to perform a specific job or overcome a

35
00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:27.110
struggle. There's a really famous study about McDonald's milkshakes. Basically,

36
00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:30.790
they wanted to increase sales, and their research revealed that morning commuters were their biggest

37
00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:35.510
buyers. But why? Well, they didn't just want a milkshake. They hired the thick milkshake to

38
00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:39.830
survive a long, really boring drive to work, mainly because it wouldn't make a mess all over

39
00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:44.550
their clothes like a bagel would, and it lasted way longer than a banana. And this brilliantly

40
00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:50.150
illustrates that a job is never just practical. There are actually three layers of intent. First

41
00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:54.150
is the functional layer, which is the practical execution, like transferring money quickly.

42
00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:59.170
But right beneath that is the emotional layer, how the user actually wants to feel,

43
00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:04.650
like perhaps feeling totally in control of their finances. And finally, you have the social layer,

44
00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:09.350
how the user wants to be perceived by others, like looking responsible to their family.

45
00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:13.170
A truly great PM builds for all three of these layers.

46
00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:16.250
You're going to want to take note of this job story formula.

47
00:04:16.710 --> 00:04:19.710
When situation, I want motivation, so result.

48
00:04:20.210 --> 00:04:23.610
Unlike your standard user story that really just focuses on a functional action,

49
00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:26.510
the job story focuses completely on motivation.

50
00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:31.070
For example, when I'm on a boring commute, I want something tasty that occupies my time

51
00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:33.310
so the drive goes fast and I'm not hungry until lunch.

52
00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:34.830
It's a total game changer.

53
00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:38.890
If you know the underlying job, you can build the absolute perfect product to do it.

54
00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:44.410
Moving right along to Section 4, Mapping the Persona Journey, Tracking the Truth.

55
00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:49.690
Look, knowing Sarah's goals is great and all, but we need to track her entire life cycle.

56
00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:52.570
A customer journey isn't just a single isolated event.

57
00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:55.290
It starts at day zero, with awareness and consideration.

58
00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:57.990
Then day one brings acquisition and onboarding.

59
00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:02.810
By week four, we're looking at usage and retention, and hopefully by year one, we hit advocacy.

60
00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:07.010
As a product manager, you must identify exactly what your persona is doing,

61
00:05:07.010 --> 00:05:10.710
what they are feeling, and what they are struggling with at every single chronological

62
00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:14.430
step on this map. That's how you ensure you are proactively meeting their needs.

63
00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:22.170
Okay, section five, passing the mom test, escaping polite lies. So asking the wrong questions during

64
00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:26.810
a user interview is actually worse than doing no research at all, seriously, because it gives

65
00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:31.350
you false confidence. You've got to be aware of polite lies. If you ask a hypothetical future

66
00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:35.450
question like, hey, would you use this feature? People are going to say yes just to be nice to

67
00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:39.910
Instead, you need grounded truth, which is drawn from the methodology of the mom test.

68
00:05:40.290 --> 00:05:42.830
You must ask about past factual experiences.

69
00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:44.510
When did you last face this problem?

70
00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:46.250
What did you actually do about it?

71
00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:48.570
Talk about their life, not your product idea.

72
00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:52.250
You have to dig into the past to validate your personas with absolute certainty.

73
00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:55.290
Let me tell you a quick story that perfectly captures this.

74
00:05:55.670 --> 00:05:58.850
A user at Vilex urgently demands an export to Excel feature.

75
00:05:59.130 --> 00:06:01.550
Now, a junior PM just goes ahead and builds it.

76
00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:03.370
But a senior PM asks why.

77
00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:09.850
They dig all the way down to the root issue and discover that the user's manager really just wants to know who is overloaded with work.

78
00:06:10.430 --> 00:06:12.930
The true job to be done there is workload visibility.

79
00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:19.530
So instead of a reactive Excel export, the PM builds a live dashboard that solves the exact same problem for dozens of clients.

80
00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:23.790
That right there? That is strategic roadmap prioritization in action.

81
00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:32.190
Which brings us to our final lesson, Section 6, Balancing Qualitative and Quantitative Data, the executive balance.

82
00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:36.050
So how exactly do we harmonize all this different data we've collected?

83
00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:38.670
Well, you need both sides of the coin to really succeed.

84
00:06:39.110 --> 00:06:40.550
On one hand, you have qualitative data.

85
00:06:40.930 --> 00:06:42.810
Those are your deep-dive interviews and observations.

86
00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:46.130
This gives you the why and helps you discover the root problems.

87
00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:49.030
But as we know, users often say one thing and do another.

88
00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:51.670
That's exactly why you need the other side, quantitative data.

89
00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:54.350
Your analytics and surveys provide the what,

90
00:06:54.590 --> 00:06:57.810
showing you actual user behavior at scale and helping you size the problem.

91
00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:00.590
Basically, qualitative insights discover the issue

92
00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:02.850
and quantitative metrics confirm its impact.

93
00:07:03.310 --> 00:07:04.930
As we wrap up this explainer,

94
00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:07.590
I want to leave you with one final kind of provocative thought.

95
00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:11.410
Users are almost always going to ask you to build a faster horse.

96
00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:14.950
But as a strategic product manager armed with deep user research,

97
00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:16.890
jobs to be done, and validated personas,

98
00:07:17.410 --> 00:07:19.090
are you really just going to build that faster horse?

99
00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:21.890
Or are you going to find out why they are riding in the first place?

100
00:07:22.370 --> 00:07:23.850
Answer that, and you'll build the car.

101
00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:26.340
Thanks so much for joining me on this deep dive.

102
00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:28.880
Now go out there and build something that truly matters.

